The key strengthening is the relationship between `disp_ena` and the horizontal counter. Concurrent assertions sample signals before the clocked updates; `s_nexttime` then checks the following clock tick. ([fpga.mit.edu](https://fpga.mit.edu/6205/_static/F25/documentation/1800-2017.pdf))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) (##1 disp_ena |-> (h_cnt < h_pixels)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "After any clock edge, disp_ena can be high only when the updated h_cnt is below h_pixels (200); the initial one-cycle delay avoids constraining uninitialized state. This invariant is preserved by each update. If disp_ena is high and rst is low, the next horizontal count is at most 200, below the sync-toggle threshold of h_pixels + h_fp (212). Thus h_sync toggles as prop requires."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

The proof hinges on a one-cycle alignment: concurrent assertions sample values at the clock edge, while this module updates its counters and outputs during that edge. The target’s `##1` delay lets the first counter/output update take effect before `disp_ena` is checked. ([accellera.org](https://www.accellera.org/images/resources/videos/SystemVerilog_Assertions_Tutorial_2016.pdf))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) 1'b1 |=> (!disp_ena || (h_cnt < h_pixels)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "After any clock edge, disp_ena is either cleared by reset or computed from the updated counters. Thus, whenever it is high, h_cnt is in the active-pixel range. The one-cycle delay avoids assuming initialized registers."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) (!rst && (h_cnt < h_pixels)) |=> (h_cnt < (h_pixels + h_fp)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "An active-range horizontal count increments by at most one. With h_pixels=200 and h_fp=12, the next count remains below the sync-pulse boundary of 212."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) (!rst) |=> ((h_cnt >= (h_pixels + h_fp)) || (h_sync != $past(h_sync))); endproperty",
      "explanation": "On a non-reset update, the RTL toggles h_sync whenever the updated horizontal count is below the sync-pulse boundary. This states the sync transition in terms of the counter, independently of disp_ena."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

Together, the lemmas take the target’s `disp_ena && !rst` condition through the counter bound to the next-cycle `h_sync` toggle; that toggle satisfies its `iff s_nexttime !h_sync` consequent.
